        <p class="profile-detail-region">Life · Central &amp; East Asia</p>
        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Tokugawa Ieyasu</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">1543 CE – 1616 CE</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Born</span><span class="info-stat-val">31 January 1543, at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/okazaki">Okazaki</a> Castle in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mikawa-province">Mikawa Province</a>, as Matsudaira Takechiyo; son of Matsudaira Hirotada, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daimyo">daimyo</a> of Mikawa of the Matsudaira clan, and Odai no Kata</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Died</span><span class="info-stat-val">1 June 1616, aged 73; the cause is thought to have been cancer or syphilis, and his physician had diagnosed stomach cancer</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Who they were</span><span class="info-stat-val"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daimyo">Daimyo</a> of Mikawa and, from 1567, head of the Tokugawa clan; ruler of the Kantō provinces under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toyotomi-hideyoshi">Toyotomi Hideyoshi</a>; one of the Council of Five Elders (1598); shōgun (1603–1605); retired shōgun, ōgosho (1605–1616)</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Known for</span><span class="info-stat-val">founding the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-shogunate">Tokugawa shogunate</a>; victory at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-sekigahara">Battle of Sekigahara</a>; making Edo, now Tokyo, the seat of power; the alliance with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oda-nobunaga">Oda Nobunaga</a>; the sieges of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osaka">Osaka</a> and the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toyotomi-clan">Toyotomi</a> line; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buke-shohatto">Buke shohatto</a> and the bakuhan system; the 1614 ban on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a></span></div>
