    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The son of a minor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daimyo">daimyo</a> who spent his childhood as a hostage, Ieyasu built his strength over four decades as ally of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oda-nobunaga">Oda Nobunaga</a> and then <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toyotomi-hideyoshi">Toyotomi Hideyoshi</a>, outlasting both.</li><li>His victory at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-sekigahara">Sekigahara</a> in 1600 and appointment as shōgun in 1603 founded a government that ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meiji-restoration">Meiji Restoration</a> in 1868.</li><li>He bound the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daimyo">daimyo</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogunate</a> by oaths, laws and redistributed domains, and destroyed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toyotomi-clan">Toyotomi</a> line at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osaka">Osaka</a> in 1615, a campaign in which George Sansom called him cruel, relentless and merciless.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>After his defeat at Mikatagahara, Ieyasu ordered the gates of his castle left open, with torches lit and drums sounding; the Takeda generals suspected a trap and did not besiege it.</p></aside>
    </section>

