    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">1543</span><span class="rf-text">Matsudaira Takechiyo, the future Ieyasu, is born at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/okazaki">Okazaki</a> Castle in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mikawa-province">Mikawa Province</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1560</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imagawa-yoshimoto">Imagawa Yoshimoto</a> is killed at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-okehazama">Battle of Okehazama</a>; Ieyasu reclaims <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/okazaki">Okazaki</a> Castle and then allies with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oda-nobunaga">Oda Nobunaga</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1582</span><span class="rf-text">After Nobunaga's death at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honno-ji">Honnō-ji</a>, Ieyasu escapes across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iga-province">Iga Province</a> to Mikawa and moves into the former Takeda lands</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1591</span><span class="rf-text">He gives up his five provinces and moves to eight provinces in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kanto-region">Kantō region</a>, making Edo his seat</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1600</span><span class="rf-text">The Eastern Army under Ieyasu defeats <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ishida-mitsunari">Ishida Mitsunari</a>'s Western Army at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-sekigahara">Battle of Sekigahara</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1603</span><span class="rf-text">Emperor Go-Yōzei gives him the title of shōgun; he passes it to his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-hidetada">Hidetada</a> in 1605</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1615</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osaka-castle">Osaka Castle</a> falls and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toyotomi-clan">Toyotomi</a> Hideyori, his mother and his infant son die, ending the Toyotomi line</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1616</span><span class="rf-text">Ieyasu dies at the age of 73 and is posthumously deified as Tōshō Daigongen</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="early">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Early Life</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/imagawa-yoshimoto"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/imagawa-yoshimoto.jpg" alt="Imagawa Yoshimoto" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/imagawa-yoshimoto">Imagawa Yoshimoto</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:ImagawaYoshimoto.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Tokugawa Ieyasu was born at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/okazaki">Okazaki</a> Castle on 31 January 1543, and was first named Matsudaira Takechiyo. His father, Matsudaira Hirotada, was a minor warlord of the Matsudaira clan who controlled part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mikawa-province">Mikawa Province</a> and a stretch of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokaido">Tōkaidō</a> highway; his mother, Odai no Kata, was the daughter of a neighbouring <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samurai">samurai</a> lord, Mizuno Tadamasa. The territory lay between stronger neighbours, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imagawa-clan">Imagawa clan</a> of Suruga to the east and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oda-clan">Oda clan</a> to the west. In the year of his birth the Matsudaira split, and when the Mizuno declared for Oda Nobuhide, Hirotada divorced Odai no Kata and sent her back to her family.</p><p>Seeking the help of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imagawa-yoshimoto">Imagawa Yoshimoto</a>, Hirotada agreed to send his heir to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunpu">Sunpu</a> as a hostage. Oda Nobuhide had the five-year-old abducted and threatened to execute him unless Hirotada broke with the Imagawa; Hirotada refused, and the boy was held for three years at the Honshōji Temple in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagoya">Nagoya</a>. Hirotada died in 1549 of unknown causes; a popular theory that he was murdered by bribed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> is called unreliable in a study by Muraoka Mikio. In 1551, after an agreement between the Imagawa general Sessai and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oda-nobunaga">Oda Nobunaga</a>, Takechiyo was taken to Sunpu, where he was treated fairly well.</p><p>He came of age in 1556, with Yoshimoto presiding, and took the name Motonobu. A year later he married Lady Tsukiyama, a relative of Yoshimoto, and became Motoyasu; their son Nobuyasu was born the next year. He fought his first battle for the Imagawa at the siege of Terabe in 1558.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="rise">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Rise</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/oda-nobunaga"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/oda-nobunaga.jpg" alt="Oda Nobunaga" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/oda-nobunaga">Oda Nobunaga</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Odanobunaga.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">狩野宗秀 (Kanō Sōshū, 1551 - 1601), Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In 1560 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imagawa-yoshimoto">Imagawa Yoshimoto</a> invaded Oda territory with 25,000 men and was killed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oda-nobunaga">Oda Nobunaga</a> in a surprise assault at Okehazama, where Motoyasu, sent to take the fort of Marune, was not present. He marched into the abandoned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/okazaki">Okazaki</a> Castle, reclaimed his ancestral seat and allied with Nobunaga, at first in secret because his wife and son were hostages at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunpu">Sunpu</a>; in 1561 he openly broke with the Imagawa and exchanged captured hostages for them. In 1563 he took the name Ieyasu. He put down the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ikko-ikki">Ikkō-ikki</a>, a movement of peasants and militant monks of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jodo-shinshu">Jōdo Shinshū</a> sect, at the Battle of Azukizaka in 1564, where bullets struck but did not pierce his armour, and by 1565 was master of all Mikawa. In 1567 he adopted the family name Tokugawa.</p><p>With <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/takeda-shingen">Takeda Shingen</a> he divided the Imagawa lands, taking Tōtōmi and in 1570 making <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamamatsu">Hamamatsu</a> his capital, and he fought beside Nobunaga against the Asakura and Azai. Shingen then turned on him and early in 1573 overwhelmed his army at Mikatagahara. In 1575 the Oda-Tokugawa forces, 38,000 strong, defeated Takeda Katsuyori at Nagashino, and in 1582 the Takeda were destroyed and Ieyasu received Suruga.</p><p>When Nobunaga died at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honno-ji">Honnō-ji</a> in 1582 Ieyasu escaped across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iga-province">Iga Province</a> and then won Kai and Shinano in a three-sided war with the Hōjō and Uesugi. In 1584 he backed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oda-nobukatsu">Oda Nobukatsu</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toyotomi-hideyoshi">Toyotomi Hideyoshi</a> and beat Hideyoshi's vanguards at Komaki and Nagakute, but after Nobukatsu surrendered he submitted, and in 1586 became Hideyoshi's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="deeds">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">What They Did</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-sekigahara"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-sekigahara.jpg" alt="Battle of Sekigahara" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-sekigahara">Battle of Sekigahara</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sekigaharascreen.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">User LordAmeth on en.wikipediaCollection of The Town of Sekigahara Archive of History and Cultural Anthropology, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>After the campaign of 1590 against the Hōjō, Ieyasu gave up his five provinces and in 1591 moved his soldiers and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> to eight provinces in the Kantō, with his seat at Edo, then an underdeveloped town. Modern Japanese historians note that the move was Hideyoshi's order; Andō Yūichirō sees it as having doubled his territory. In 1598 the dying Hideyoshi named him one of five regents for the child <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toyotomi-clan">Toyotomi</a> Hideyori, the most powerful of them. Opposition gathered around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ishida-mitsunari">Ishida Mitsunari</a>, and on 21 October 1600, Ieyasu's Eastern Army, initially 75,000 men, met a Western Army of 120,000 at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-sekigahara">Sekigahara</a>. Ieyasu had for months promised land and leniency to western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daimyo">daimyo</a> who changed sides, and the battle ended in an overwhelming victory.</p><p>He redistributed the domains: allies were enlarged, some western houses destroyed, and Hideyori reduced to an ordinary daimyo. On 24 March 1603, Emperor Go-Yōzei gave him the title of shōgun. He abdicated in 1605 in favour of his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-hidetada">Hidetada</a>, showing that the office would be hereditary, but as retired shōgun at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunpu">Sunpu</a> remained the effective ruler. He supervised the building of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edo-castle">Edo Castle</a>, the largest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, at the expense of the other daimyo, and accepted the rule that each lord maintain a residence in Edo.</p><p>In 1611 and 1612 the daimyo swore to his Three Laws. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuge">Kuge</a> shohatto of 1613 placed the court nobles under strict supervision, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buke-shohatto">Buke shohatto</a> of 1615 allowed each daimyo only one castle. He dealt with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, often consulting the English pilot William Adams.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="opponents">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Opponents &amp; Critics</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/ikko-ikki"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ikko-ikki.jpg" alt="Ikkō-ikki" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/ikko-ikki">Ikkō-ikki</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Turumaru_Flags.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">投稿者がファイル作成, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Ieyasu's enemies in war were the Imagawa, the Takeda, the Hōjō and finally the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toyotomi-clan">Toyotomi</a> party; within his own house the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ikko-ikki">Ikkō-ikki</a> rising drew away some of his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a>, and Ishikawa Kazumasa defected to Hideyoshi in 1585. His conduct included acts recorded as atrocities. After taking Horikawa Castle in 1569 he ordered the prisoners and residents massacred, women and children among them; around 700 people are recorded as beheaded. In 1579, when Nobunaga accused Lady Tsukiyama and Nobuyasu of conspiring with Takeda Katsuyori, Ieyasu had his wife executed and forced his son to commit seppuku. The account in the Mikawa Monogatari is thought implausible by modern historians, and Katsuhiro Taniguchi prefers a theory of conflict between factions at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamamatsu">Hamamatsu</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/okazaki">Okazaki</a>.</p><p>After the winter siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osaka">Osaka</a> in 1614 the Tokugawa filled in the castle's outer moats once an armistice was agreed, and attacked again. According to George Sansom he was cruel, relentless and merciless towards the Toyotomi survivors: for days scores of men and women were hunted down and executed, among them an eight-year-old son of Hideyori, who was beheaded. Hamada Koichiro records that historians after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meiji-restoration">Meiji Restoration</a> portrayed him as a “cunning old man” who used a temple bell as a pretext for war. Watanabe Daimon, by contrast, stresses his readiness to forgive enemies and vassals who had betrayed him.</p><p>At first tolerant of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>, he turned against it after the Okamoto Daihachi incident of 1612 and in 1614 signed an edict banning the religion and expelling foreign missionaries.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="last">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Last Years</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/yodo-dono"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/yodo-dono.jpg" alt="Yodo-dono" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/yodo-dono">Yodo-dono</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Yodo-dono_cropped.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">奈良県立美術館収蔵『傳 淀殿畫像』 / Nara Museum of Art, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The last threat to Ieyasu's rule was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toyotomi-clan">Toyotomi</a> Hideyori, the son and heir of Hideyoshi, a young <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daimyo">daimyo</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osaka-castle">Osaka Castle</a> around whom <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samurai">samurai</a> opposed to the Tokugawa gathered. Ieyasu found fault with the opening ceremony of a temple built by Hideyori, as though it prayed for his death and the ruin of his house, and ordered him to leave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osaka">Osaka</a>; those in the castle refused. In 1614 Ieyasu and the shōgun <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-hidetada">Hidetada</a> besieged the castle. An <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armistice-of-11-november-1918">armistice</a> followed after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannon">cannon</a> fire threatened Hideyori's mother, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yodo-dono">Yodo-dono</a>. When Hideyori refused a further order to leave, Ieyasu attacked again with 155,000 soldiers. The castle fell in 1615 and nearly all its defenders were killed, including Hideyori, his mother and his infant son; Ieyasu refused the plea of Hideyori's wife <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senhime">Senhime</a>, his own granddaughter, for their lives.</p><p>Ieyasu died in 1616 at the age of 73. The cause is thought to have been cancer or syphilis. His chief physician, Sotetsu Katayama, diagnosed stomach cancer, but Ieyasu took the illness for a tapeworm, refused the medicine prepared for him and kept to his own remedies, while his health declined; when his son Hidetada also warned him, he was angered, and he exiled the physician. He had wished to be deified after death to protect his descendants, and was given the name Tōshō Daigongen. His remains were buried at Kunōzan; many believe that they were reburied at Nikkō after the first anniversary of his death, but neither shrine has opened the graves. At his death his personal wealth was estimated at about 4 million koku, and he possessed about 42 tons of gold.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/hayashi-razan"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hayashi-razan.jpg" alt="Hayashi Razan" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/hayashi-razan">Hayashi Razan</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E6%9E%97%E7%BE%85%E5%B1%B1_%E5%83%8F.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-shogunate">Tokugawa shogunate</a>, the third shogunal government after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamakura">Kamakura</a> and the Ashikaga, ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> for the next 260 years and opened the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edo-period">Edo period</a>. Edo became the centre of political power and the de facto capital, though <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyoto">Kyoto</a> remained the seat of the emperor; the Imperial Palace now stands on the site of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edo-castle">Edo Castle</a>. The system known as bakuhan used graded rewards and punishments to make the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daimyo">daimyo</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samurai">samurai</a> live in peace under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogunate</a>. The lords who had pledged themselves before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-sekigahara">Sekigahara</a> became the fudai daimyō and those who did so afterwards the tozama, regarded as inferior. In 1639 his successors enacted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sakoku">sakoku</a> policy, which closed Japan to the outside world, with some exceptions, for over 200 years.</p><p>Ieyasu is counted the third of the three “Great Unifiers” of Japan, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oda-nobunaga">Oda Nobunaga</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toyotomi-hideyoshi">Toyotomi Hideyoshi</a>. His shrines at Kunōzan and Nikkō gave their name to the gongen style of architecture. A devotee of the Jōdo-shū school of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a>, he moved his family temple to Zōjō-ji in Edo and later patronised Confucian scholars such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hayashi-razan">Hayashi Razan</a>.</p><p>Assessments differ. Junji Mitsunari traces his boldness to a childhood of hardship as a hostage; John T. Kuehn sees a master of long strategies; Kazuto Hongō credits him with stability built on the use of his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a>' abilities and on systems of succession rather than on single individuals. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meiji-restoration">Meiji Restoration</a> historians tended to present him as cunning. His life served as the model for Lord Toranaga in James Clavell's novel Shōgun.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Tokugawa Ieyasu (1543–1616) rose from hostage son of a minor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daimyo">daimyo</a> to ally of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oda-nobunaga">Oda Nobunaga</a> and leading <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toyotomi-hideyoshi">Toyotomi Hideyoshi</a>. Victory at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-sekigahara">Sekigahara</a> in 1600 made him master of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, and as shōgun from 1603 he founded a government that lasted until 1868. He destroyed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toyotomi-clan">Toyotomi</a> line at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osaka">Osaka</a> in 1615.</em></p>

